description:
I had a shiny app in a project subfolder, with global.R, server.R, and ui.R. I got several deployment issues (manifest, project folder not found, app.R not found, etc). I was using the supposedly simple Posit Publisher extension in Positron. 

1) It shouldn't be hard;
2) the error messages should be more informative.

## Problem

The TypeScript `ConnectAPI` client (`packages/connect-api/src/client.ts`) does not handle HTTP cookies. In high-availability Connect deployments with a load balancer, this causes unpredictable 404 errors during `waitForTask` polling.

**Root cause:** Load balancers in front of HA Connect clusters typically set a session affinity cookie on the first response, ensuring subsequent requests from the same client are routed to the same backend node. The Go client handles this correctly — it creates an `http.Client` with a `cookiejar` (`internal/clients/http_client/http_client.go:290`), so affinity cookies are automatically stored and resent. The TypeScript axios client has no cookie handling, so each poll request can land on a different backend server. If the task state hasn't replicated yet, the server returns a 404.

**Symptom:** Intermittent 404 errors from `GET /__api__/v1/tasks/{taskId}` during the `waitForTask` polling loop after `deployBundle`. More likely to occur on HA Connect installations behind a load balancer.

## Suggested approach

Add axios request/response interceptors in the `ConnectAPI` constructor to capture `set-cookie` headers and resend them on subsequent requests:

```typescript
let cookies: string[] = [];

this.client.interceptors.response.use((response) => {
  const setCookie = response.headers["set-cookie"];
  if (setCookie) {
    cookies = setCookie;
  }
  return response;
});

this.client.interceptors.request.use((config) => {
  if (cookies.length > 0) {
    config.headers["Cookie"] = cookies.join("; ");
  }
  return config;
});
```

Since `ConnectAPI` is a single-origin client (all requests go to one Connect server), full RFC 6265 cookie parsing with domain/path scoping isn't necessary. Simple header forwarding is sufficient and avoids adding a dependency like `tough-cookie`.

## How the Go client handles this

`internal/clients/http_client/http_client.go:290-312`:
```go
cookieJar, err := cookiejar.New(&cookiejar.Options{
    PublicSuffixList: publicsuffix.List,
})
// ...
return &http.Client{
    Jar:       cookieJar,
    Timeout:   timeout,
    Transport: authTransport,
}
```

## Description
Now that Connect supports overriding repository resolution, we should add support to publisher to set this in manifest files. The options are `lax`, `strict`, `legacy`, and `lockfile`.

```
...   
"environment": { 
  "r": { 
    ...
    "package_repository_resolution": "lockfile" 
  } 
}  
...
```

In Connect Cloud, a user may be a member of multiple accounts. We need to support being able to choose one if there are multiple through the API.

Proposed contract:
```
GET /connect-cloud/accounts
Connect-Cloud-Base-Url: https://api.staging.connect.posit.cloud
Authorization: Bearer {token}

// response body
{
  "accounts": [
    {
      "id": "uuid",
      "name": "mslynch",
      "permissionToPublish": true
    }
  ]
}
```

### Description

Add a GET endpoint to fetch the user's accounts:
Response body:
```
 {
   "accounts": [
     {
       "id": "uuid",
       "name": "mslynch",
       "permission_to_publish": true
     }
   ]
}
```

* DESCRIPTION file (i.e. Depends: R (>= 3.5.0)
* renv.lock specifies R -> Version (debatable, we should discuss)

The version constraint should be set within `environment` -> `r` -> `requires` in the `manifest.json`.  If there is no version constraint found, do not specify anything in `environment` -> `r` -> `requires`. Either way, we should continue to populate `platform` with the "current" R version.

Example `manifest.json`:

```
{
  "version": 1,
  "locale": "en_US",
  "platform": "4.3.1",
  "metadata": {
    "appmode": "api",
    "primary_rmd": null,
    "primary_html": null,
    "content_category": null,
    "has_parameters": false
  },
  "environment": {
    "r": {
      "requires": ">=4.2.0, <5.0"
    }
  },
  "packages": {
    ...
  }
}
```

Probably not wanting to expose this flow, since the new destination flow is more straightforward?

Or do we want to support this as a Command Pallete command only?

Currently flow is:

  // Name the deployment
  // Select the credential to use, if there is more than one
  // Select the config file to use, if there are more than one
  // IF parameter 'allowPublish' is true, prompt to deploy
  // IF prompt to deploy was Yes, then call publish API
  // return name of deployment file

Is launched from Deployment view:

  // Select the credential to use, if there is more than one
  // Select the config file to use, if there are more than one
  // result in calling publish API

This should probably be deprecated. We'll be adding a new deployment command that mimics the destination control more.

Should use `box-sizing: border-box`. It is the more intuitive box-sizing model and will avoid CSS issues.

```
*, *::before, *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
```

Version: 0.0.dev6-113-g8de8e33a

Deploying either `Jupyter` or `Voila` notebooks from Windows via UI, I get this response from the configurations API (missing `title`):

```
[
    {
        "configurationName": "default",
        "configurationPath": ".posit\\publish\\default.toml",
        "configuration": {
            "$schema": "https://cdn.posit.co/publisher/schemas/posit-publishing-schema-v3.json",
            "type": "jupyter-notebook",
            "entrypoint": "stock-report-jupyter.ipynb",
            "validate": true,
            "python": {
                "version": "3.11.5",
                "packageFile": "requirements.txt",
                "packageManager": "pip"
            }
        }
    }
]
```

So in Connect they show up as "Untitled".

It works as expected using the CLI in Windows.

Deploying the same noteook from macOS in Chrome/Safari/Firefox I get a `title`:

```
[
    {
        "configurationName": "default",
        "configurationPath": ".posit/publish/default.toml",
        "configuration": {
            "$schema": "https://cdn.posit.co/publisher/schemas/posit-publishing-schema-v3.json",
            "type": "jupyter-notebook",
            "entrypoint": "stock_report_jupyter.ipynb",
            "validate": true,
            "title": "long-running-cell-notebook",
            "python": {
                "version": "3.11.6",
                "packageFile": "requirements.txt",
                "packageManager": "pip"
            }
        }
    }
]
```

I also get a title when deploying non-Notebook content types from Windows:
```
[
    {
        "configurationName": "default",
        "configurationPath": ".posit\\publish\\default.toml",
        "configuration": {
            "$schema": "https://cdn.posit.co/publisher/schemas/posit-publishing-schema-v3.json",
            "type": "python-fastapi",
            "entrypoint": "main.py",
            "validate": true,
            "title": "stock-api-fastapi",
            "python": {
                "version": "3.11.5",
                "packageFile": "requirements.txt",
                "packageManager": "pip"
